            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to yoke, then the Latin for to unyoke."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: habēna, habēnae",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "habēna, habēnae — \"a rein; a strap\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Raedārius habēnās tenet et equī lentē eunt. The coachman holds the reins and the horses go slowly."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "First declension, feminine: habēna, habēnae f., mostly plural: habēnās dare, to give a horse its head."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "habēna is built on habeō 'to have, to hold' (EtymDB; Lewis & Short): the strap by which a horse is held."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
