          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "frēnum, frēnī — \"a bridle, a bit\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Equus ferōx frēnum nōn amat. A wild horse does not like the bridle."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Second declension, neuter: frēnum, frēnī n.; the plural is often frēnī m. Also spelled fraenum. English refrain 'to hold back' comes from refrēnāre 'to bridle back'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "frēnum is inherited and is usually traced to PIE dʰer- 'to hold, to support' (EtymDB; Lewis & Short). English refrain 'to hold back' comes from Latin refrēnāre, built on it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
